flowchart LR

HARN["`The harness — why these numbers mean anything`"]
  H1["`Locust · perf/ · 28 runs, CSV + HTML in the repo`"]
HARN -.- H1
  H2["`forges a DISTINCT IP per request
otherwise my own per-IP rate limiter
becomes the bottleneck of my own test`"]
  H3["`AI paths excluded and disabled
mixing LLM latency into a CRUD average
produces a number that describes nothing`"]
  H4["`realistic think time: one click / 5–10s
because the real question is
'how many PEOPLE fit', not 'how many loops'`"]

MEAS["`MEASURED — I have the report`"]
  M1["`250 users → 33 req/s · p95 26ms · 0% · idle`"]
MEAS -.- M1
  M2["`500 users → 65 req/s · p95 46ms · 0% · comfortable`"]
  M3["`750 users → 99 req/s · p95 545ms · 0% ← RECOMMENDED CEILING`"]
  M4["`1000 users → 120 req/s · p95 1.1s · 0.2% ← first errors`"]
  M5["`1500 users → 89% failures ← the wall
p95 DROPS to 290ms here: connections rejected
instantly, not served slowly — kernel resetting
a full accept backlog, nothing reaches my code`"]
  M6["`NEVER DIED: Restarts=0 · OOMKilled=false
224MB of 1GB · healthy in EVERY run,
including the 89%-failure one
it degrades and refuses. it does not fall over.`"]
  M1 --> M2 --> M3 --> M4 --> M5
  M5 --- M6

PROOF["`THE PROOF IT SCALES — same box, same 4 CPUs, same load`"]
  W1["`1 worker  → 3,911 reqs · 66 req/s · p95 3200ms · 0 fail`"]
PROOF -.- W1
  W4["`4 workers → 8,279 reqs · 140 req/s · p95 1200ms · 0 fail`"]
  WD["`2.11x throughput · p95 2.67x BETTER
only change: '--workers 4' · zero app code

req/s PER WORKER is nearly identical in both —
4 cores to ONE uvicorn process does nothing,
because one process uses one core`"]
  W1 --> W4 --> WD

END["`ENDURANCE — throughput without it is worthless`"]
  E1["`112,549 reqs · 9 min · 208 req/s steady
ZERO failures · p95 75ms
memory 510→570MB in minute 1 (workers warming),
then FLAT 568–571MB for 8 minutes
no leak · no restart · no OOM`"]
END -.- E1
  E2["`SOAK: 1,263,978 reqs · 3h34m
300 concurrent · 0.26% failures`"]
  E3["`2,455,264 requests across all 28 runs`"]
  E1 --> E2 --> E3

COEF["`THE COEFFICIENT — this is the only measured conversion

750 users produced 99 req/s → 0.13 req/s PER USER

everything below is multiplication, and I say so out loud`"]

CALC["`CALCULATED — arithmetic, not measurement`"]
  C1["`80,000 users × 0.13 ≈ 10,600 req/s
÷180 req/s per worker ≈ 60 cores
+40% headroom ≈ 84 cores ≈ 21 containers of 4 vCPU`"]
CALC -.- C1
  C2["`1,000,000 users × 0.13 ≈ 130,000 req/s
÷180 ≈ 722 cores
+40% headroom ≈ 1,010 cores ≈ 250 containers`"]
  C3["`the 40% is not padding: bursts, rolling deploys,
and one whole availability zone going away`"]
  C1 --> C2 --> C3

SPREAD["`WHERE LOAD IS SPREAD — four levels, four problems`"]
  S1["`1 · EDGE — Traefik
loadbalancer.server.port=8000
adding a replica = adding a container`"]
SPREAD -.- S1
  S2["`2 · IN-CONTAINER — UVICORN_WORKERS
the 2.11x multiplier · one env var
the cheapest lever in the system`"]
  S3["`3 · DATABASE — PgBouncer, transaction mode
makes NOTHING faster today
preventive: it exists so 1 and 2 can grow`"]
  S4["`4 · BACKGROUND — Celery fan-out
a 40-item plan = 40 independent tasks
across every worker`"]
  S5["`5 · (opposite direction) LLM semaphore = 5
limiting my OWN outbound calls,
because blowing the provider quota
is DDoSing yourself`"]

BREAK["`WHAT BREAKS FIRST — in order. the API is the EASY part.`"]
  B0["`WHY the API is easy: it is STATELESS.
session is a JWT cookie, not server memory →
any replica serves any user →
throughput is bought with copies.
The hard part is what those copies SHARE.`"]
BREAK -.- B0
  B1["`1 · POSTGRES CONNECTIONS — breaks TODAY
max_connections=100 · each API process opens up to 30
→ THREE processes before refusal, long before CPU
60 workers would want 1,800 raw connections
FIX: PgBouncer (already in the stack), app pool → ~5`"]
  B2["`2 · POSTGRES READ CAPACITY
the mix is read-heavy: /subjects /plans /auth/me
FIX: read replicas (GET→replica, write→primary) +
short-TTL Redis cache. The Service pattern makes this
ONE change in ONE place instead of every router.`"]
  B3["`3 · REDIS CONTENTION
rate limit + cache + broker on one instance
FIX: split the roles. >100k ops/s per instance,
so this is tuning, not redesign.`"]
  B4["`4 · ARGON2 UNDER A LOGIN STORM
expensive on CPU BY DESIGN — that is the point.
Invisible in steady state (15-min cookie, rare logins).
But 80k people at 9am is a spike of its own shape.
FIX: auth-dedicated replica pool + tune the cost
parameter against measurement.
The harness has AUTH_WEIGHT to produce it on purpose.`"]
  B0 --> B1 --> B2 --> B3 --> B4

FLAW["`MY OWN SYSTEM'S FLAW — I present it before you find it

an exhausted DB pool returns 500 today. It should be
503 + Retry-After, because it is BACKPRESSURE, not an error.
Consequence 1: client and LB never learn to back off.
Consequence 2 (worse): on the dashboard those 500s are
indistinguishable from a real bug — I polluted my own
error signal with load.
Written in perf/RESULTS.md. One status code, one handler.`"]

VERD["`THE HONEST VERDICT

Can it reach 1M? YES — stateless API + managed Postgres/Redis.
10k req/s is a medium deployment, not an exotic one.
By adding replicas alone? NO — connections and read capacity
break first, and those two ARE the engineering project.
Rewrite? NO — infrastructure + 3 small code changes:
a pool setting, one cache, one status code.

I did NOT test 1M. Nobody does from one laptop.
I tested the COEFFICIENT, and found it linear.`"]

HARN ==> MEAS
MEAS ==> PROOF
PROOF ==> END
END ==> COEF
COEF ==> CALC
CALC ==> SPREAD
SPREAD ==> BREAK
BREAK --- FLAW
BREAK ==> VERD

